   187	
   188	## 3. The sub-chart collapse principle
   189	
   190	**Proposition SUBCHART.** Let `R` be a subset of the residual variables and let
   191	`S` be the coordinate sub-chart on which the residual variables outside `R` are
   192	set to `0`.  Let `Sem(R)` be the numerical semigroup generated by the weights of
   193	`R`.  Then on `S` every eliminated variable whose weight lies outside `Sem(R)`
   194	restricts to `0`, and the others restrict to `A_t`-combinations of the monomials
   195	in `R` of the given weight.
   196	
   197	*Proof.* By Section 2 the solved value of each eliminated variable is weighted
   198	homogeneous, in the residual variables, of that variable's weight.  Setting the
   199	complementary variables to zero deletes every monomial that involves them; what
   200	remains is spanned by monomials in `R` of that weight, and there are none when
   201	the weight is outside `Sem(R)`. `[]`
   202	
   203	The bound is sharp and, when the weight admits a unique monomial, the solved
   204	value is forced to be one `A_t`-scalar times that monomial.  `c4_subchart_check.py`
   205	controls this on a *second* sub-chart, `R = {q_(t-1,0), b3}`: it restricts all
   206	`2t+1` high pivots of the charged records and checks the surviving support
   207	against the semigroup prediction, printing `SUBCHART2_t = PASS` at `t=4` and
   208	`t=5`.  Both index collisions are exercised there: `b2` (weight `2t+1`) survives
   209	at `t=4` because `2t+1 = 3(t-1)` exactly then, and vanishes at `t=5`, both as
   210	predicted.  (The `b1` and `T(0)` right-hand sides are recorded before the high
   211	substitutions, so they are still functions of not-yet-eliminated variables;
   212	their grading is checked by `a4_weights.py` instead.)
   213	
   214	This is where the present route separates from band-index induction: it does
   215	not iterate over bands at all.  It chooses a sub-chart in which the triangular
   216	solve degenerates, and then the whole spine is available in closed form.
   217	
   218	## 4. The b3-axis theorem
   219	
   220	Take `R = {b3}`, `wt(b3)=t+1`, so `S = {b4=0, q_(2,0)=...=q_(t-1,0)=0}` and
   350	This makes the `t=2` behaviour a derived consequence of one closed formula, the
   351	factor `(t-2)` shared by (4.5) and (4.6), rather than a sampled exception.  It
   352	also settles a structural question the charged report left open: the two-lemma
   353	split (8.2) is *strictly stronger* than (8.1), and any induction that tries to
