   221	the norms `F_C(t,j)`, `F_Q(t,j)` of (5.11)/(5.14).  The exact b4-axis
   222	coefficient of the first pivot `C_1 = gamma_(t,1) b4` has denominator equal
   223	to the odd part of `F_C(t,1)`:
   224	
   225	| `t` | `F_C(t,1)` | denominator of `gamma_(t,1)` |
   226	|---:|---|---:|
   227	| 3 | `2^4 5^2 59` | 295 |
   228	| 4 | `2^3 3 5^2 113` | 565 |
   229	| 5 | `2^3 3 73 89` | 6497 |
   230	| 6 | `2^4 7 17 163` | 2771 |
   231	| 7 | `2^5 3 5813` | 5813 |
   232	
   233	This is a theorem, not an observation.  **Near-axis uniformity
   234	(PROVED-HERE).**  Fix `W>=0`.  For `t>W` the coefficient in `T_(t,k)` of any
   235	monomial `b3^a m` with `m` a monomial of weight `W` in `(b4,q_2,0,...)` is an
   236	element of `Q(t)[d]` whose denominator divides `prod_(j<=W) F_C(t,j)` times
   237	the listed rational diagonals: by the grading, only the pivots of weight
   238	`<=W` (namely `C_1,...,C_W`) and the axis data of section 3 can enter, and
   239	each is a fixed finite recurrence step in `Q(t)[d]`.  The first instance
   240	(`weight1_closed_form.py`, weight-1 linearization of (2.1)) is
   241	
   242	```text
   243	C_1 |_(b4-axis) = gamma_(t,1) b4,
   244	gamma_(t,1) = (-12dt^2+6dt+72t^3+22t^2-3t+2) / (48t^3+20t^2-t+2),
   245	F_C(t,1) = 4(t+1)(48t^3+20t^2-t+2),                                     (4.1)
   246	```
   247	
   248	and the same linearization returns the band-`4t` diagonal equal to
   249	`p_C(t,1)` (ratio `1`), an independent re-derivation of (5.10) at `j=1`.
   250	Formula (4.1) reproduces the table above exactly at `t=3,...,7`.
   251	
   252	Deeper pivots and every pure-`b4` row coefficient (`ROW_B4AXIS` in
   253	`ax_t*.out`; 60-digit numerators at `t=3`, 100-digit at `t=4`) accumulate
   254	products of these norms.  Since `F_C(t,j)` is an irreducible quartic in
   255	`(t,j)` and the number of pivots is `2t+1`, the b4-axis values `tau_(t,k)` are
   256	a `t`-indexed sequence whose denominators grow with `t`; they are not
   257	elements of `Q(t)[d]` (the offset `4t+1-k` of the pure `b4` power grows with
   258	`t`, so the uniformity statement above does not reach them).  The same holds on the `q_2,0`-axis (denominators
   259	1201 at `t=3`, 1669 at `t=4` from `F_C(t,2)`).  The correct uniform statement
   260	is therefore the recurrence (2.1)-(2.3) with the specialization denominator
   261	
   262	```text
   263	D_spine(t) = 60 (2t+1)^5 * prod_(j=1)^(t-1) F_C(t,j) * prod_(j=t)^(2t) F_Q(t,j)
   264	             * prod_j (t-j)(4t-2j+1) * (t+1)(3t+2) * 12q^2(t+1)(4t+1),
   265	```
   266	
   267	which has no positive-integer root (every factor is positive on its index
   268	range; `F_C(t,t-n)`, `F_Q(t,q-n)` are the manifestly positive forms (5.11),
   269	(5.14)).  A generic-`t` unit certificate for (8.1) would in addition need a
   270	`t`-uniform description of the *rows*, which does not exist in this sense;
   271	this is the FALLACY-v2 reason a fixed-size certificate cannot be interpolated
   272	from `t<=7`.
   273	
   274	## 5. Exact fixed-`t` results (task 5, and new kills)
   275	
   276	For each `t` the weighted ring is `A_t[b3,b4,q_2,0..q_(t-1),0]` with
   277	`wp(t+1,1,2,...,t-1)`.  Tests (`analyze_rows.py`):
   278	
   279	```text
   280	JPLUS: dim <T_1..T_(2t-1)>            (0  =>  V = {0}  =>  (8.1))
   281	T0HOM: least N with (T^hom_0)^N in J_+ (radical membership; the exact criterion)
   282	RESZ : dim <T_1..T_(2t-1), b4>         (0  <=> RESIDUAL-ZERO)
   283	TOPT : least N with b4^N in <T_t..T_(2t-1)>  (exists <=> TOP-TAIL-UNIT)
   284	```
   285	
   286	### 5.1 Promotion lemma (PROVED-HERE)
   287	
   288	Let `R` be a discrete valuation ring with fraction field `K` and residue field
   289	`kappa`, and `J` an ideal of `R[x_1,...,x_n]` generated by polynomials that
   290	are homogeneous for positive integer weights.  If the affine cone
   291	`V(J kappa[x])` is `{0}` over `kappabar`, then `V(J K[x])` is `{0}` over
   292	`Kbar`.  Proof: `X=Proj(R[x]/J)` is a closed subscheme of a weighted
   293	projective space over `Spec R`, hence proper over `Spec R`; its image is
   294	closed.  The special fibre is `Proj(kappa[x]/J)`, empty exactly when the
   295	cone modulo the maximal ideal is `{0}`; so the image misses the closed point,
   296	and the only closed subset of `Spec R` missing the closed point is empty.
   297	Hence `X` is empty and the generic fibre `Proj(K[x]/J)` is empty, i.e. the
   298	cone over `K` is `{0}`.
   299	
   300	Application.  Fix `t`, a prime `p` not dividing `12q^2` with a root `r` of
   301	`H_t` modulo `p`, and the prime `P=(p, y-r)` of `Z[y]/(H_t)`.  The rows
   302	`T_(t,k)` computed in `A_t` have coefficients `a+by` with `a,b` rational;
   303	Singular's evaluation of these at `y=r` in `GF(p)` fails loudly on a
   304	denominator divisible by `p` (`divzero_test.sing`: `? div. by 0` and
   305	`? error occurred`), and no such marker occurs in any modular `.out/.err`
   306	file used here (scanned for `div. by 0`, `div by 0`, `error occurred`).  Thus the rows lie in `R[x]` for the localisation `R` at
   307	`P`, whose fraction field is `A_t` (a field for non-split `t`; for split `t`
   308	each rational fibre is treated with `R=Z_(p)`), and the modular `JPLUS
   309	dim=0` on one root is a characteristic-zero proof that
   310	`V(T_(t,1),...,T_(t,2t-1))={0}`, hence of (8.1) and of (T) at that `t`.  This
   311	is the *safe* direction: an inhomogeneous unit ideal modulo `p` proves
   312	nothing (FALLACY-v2), but emptiness of a proper family is an open condition
   313	on the base.  Negative control: at `t=2`, `y=1/5`, the cone has dimension one
   314	exactly (the b3-axis) and dimension one modulo `32003`; the lemma's
   315	contrapositive (nonempty generic fibre forces nonempty special fibre) is
   316	satisfied.
   317	
